   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "when it is defined as <code>int malloc()</code>, does it replicate <code>void *malloc(size_t)</code>?"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 24691068,
   "parent_id": 7050798,
   "author_user_id": 3828152,
   "author_display_name": "user3828152",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/24691068",
   "score": -4,
   "created_utc": "2014-07-11T06:08:11Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2014-07-11T06:08:11Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>The only solution for such warnings is to include stdlib.h in the program.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 24957308,
   "parent_id": 7050798,
   "author_user_id": 3877220,
   "author_display_name": "santosh sahu",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/24957308",
   "score": 5,
   "created_utc": "2014-07-25T13:49:34Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2017-12-10T21:54:30Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2017-12-10T21:54:30Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>The stdlib.h file contains the header information or prototype of the malloc, calloc, realloc and free functions.</p>\n\n<p>So to avoid this warning in ANSI C, you should include the stdlib header file.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 48513865,
   "parent_id": 7050809,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 1322972,
   "author_display_name": "WhozCraig",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/7050798/#comment48513865_7050809",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2015-05-13T01:54:22Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "@user1343318 Not necessarily, which is precisely the reason for the warning. Ex: a x64 platform with 64-bit data pointers and 32-bit <code>int</code> values will puke goat feces, while a x86 32bit-data-pointer/32bit-<code>int</code> can seemingly work correctly. <i>Neither</i> are correct, as in neither case is the compiler aware of what <code>malloc</code> actually returns, and assumes <code>int</code> in response."
  },
